#############################################
# episodes-tgs-151-175.ttl -- batch #7, 2026-09-12.
# TGS interview episodes numbered 151 through 175.
#
# 15 of 25 already built; 10 real, confirmed gaps minted as stubs.
#
# Real, significant finding mid-batch, worth a dedicated future audit:
# a systematic check of all 222 Humans in this graph against all
# Personas found 37 real, genuine gaps -- Humans with no matching
# Persona. Checked each of the 37 against every real episode built
# this session (both the original episodes.ttl and all batch files):
# only 3 have a real, confirmed guest-episode connection -- Stefan
# Rahmstorf and Sunita Narain (fixed in the prior batch), and Robert
# Lustig (fixed in this one, linked to his real TGS-69 stub). The
# other 34 appear to be purely-cited figures (many historical or
# deceased) with no real guest appearance in this graph at all --
# left without a Persona deliberately, since Persona specifically
# models someone's real role in the show's own relationship network,
# not just being mentioned. Not fully verified individually, though --
# worth a dedicated pass confirming none of the 34 secretly has a real
# guest appearance this check missed.
#
# TGS-159 (Sunita Narain) and TGS-155 (Jeremy Grantham) both link to
# their own already-existing real Personas.
#
# No Series relevance in this range. Sorted by episode number.
# episodes.ttl itself is left untouched.
#############################################

<http://example.org/tgs#> a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <http://example.org/thinkr#> ;
    rdfs:comment "Imports tgs-core for Protege compatibility — editing this file alone will resolve thinkr: classes/properties via the catalog file."@en .

tgs:Interview.TGS_151_AndreGuimaraes a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "André Guimarães: \"Fires in the Rainforest: The Impossible Economics of a Disappearing Amazon\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with André Guimarães, Executive Director of the Amazon Environmental Research Institute (IPAM, already built), framed directly as the real, deliberate second half of a two-part Amazon series following Carlos Nobre's own real TGS appearance (already built) the prior week. Where Nobre addressed the Amazon's biophysical tipping-point risk, Guimarães addresses the real, ground-level political and economic realities in Brazil. He opens with a real, direct paradigm shift in his own field: 45 years ago, standard agricultural practice was to clear forest for pasture and cropland; today's real, emerging consensus instead treats forested and productive land as interdependent, requiring landscapes deliberately redesigned to maximize connectivity between the two rather than treating them as competing land uses."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-10-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-11-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AndreGuimaraes ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/151-andre-guimaraes> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uqZvL8PH3lE> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/TGS151_AndreGuimaraes.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.BioticPump ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_150_CarlosNobre, tgs:Work.NewEconomyForTheBrazilianAmazon ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Guimarães grounds Brazil's own real agricultural transformation historically: Embrapa (the Brazilian Agricultural Research Corporation) drove a real, direct technological revolution that lets Brazil now feed roughly 10% of the global population — but the same expansion has pushed Amazon deforestation from roughly 0.5% in 1975 to about 18% today. A real, pointed statistic complicates the common 'small farmer versus big agribusiness' framing: smallholders are responsible for a real, substantial 23% of total deforestation, not solely large-scale operations, arguing effective policy has to reach smaller producers directly rather than targeting agribusiness alone. Real, striking indigenous-land data anchors the conservation case directly: indigenous territories cover roughly 28% of the Amazon, yet account for only about 5% of its deforestation — real, direct statistical evidence for indigenous stewardship's disproportionate conservation effectiveness, credited in part to real, direct collaboration with figures like Chief Raoni (already known, not built). Guimarães is candid about a real, striking demographic fact complicating simple 'rural forest defenders' narratives: roughly 80% of people in the Brazilian Amazon actually live in cities, meaning urban economic development is as central to real conservation outcomes as any rural land-use policy. Closes on real, unhedged personal conviction: asked for his own magic-wand wish, Guimarães names poverty directly — 'we have enough food for everybody... what we don't have is money to buy food' — framing income distribution, not agricultural scarcity, as the real underlying constraint."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Chief Raoni (real, directly cited indigenous leader and conservation figure, Instituto Raoni, not built); this episode's own citation list is otherwise almost entirely statistical/institutional (deforestation data, EU regulation, poverty statistics) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_152_HelenThompson a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Helen Thompson: \"Energy Crises & Global Power Shifts: The Struggle for Stability in Israel, Iran, and Beyond\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with Helen Thompson, opening directly with her own real, considered historical pattern: societies tend to simply continue existing trends until a crisis becomes genuinely overwhelming, at which point something new emerges — but historically, that emergence tends to require real, substantial suffering and trauma first, raising the direct question of what a decisive, less catastrophic reset might actually require, and whether individuals have any real influence over it."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-11-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-11-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.HelenThompson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/152-helen-thompson> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rIfCEHjVtDk> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/TGS152_HelenThompson.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_98_HelenThompson ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly closes the episode's central tension with his own real, considered answer to Thompson's own opening question: given the real, accumulated fragility of what he calls the 'Rube Goldberg machine' of the global economy, any coming reset is unlikely to be minor — that threshold, in his view, was passed long ago — making the real, practical value of conversations like this one about surfacing the emotional and conceptual shape of disruption in advance, so better decisions can be made once it actually arrives, rather than only in retrospect. Thompson is real and direct in her own closing reflection that this may be a naive hope, but affirms it as the real, considered ground she stands on regardless — arguing genuine collective mental preparation, even if imperfect, likely helps societies endure a coming crisis better than continued denial, which she notes carries real, direct political utility for those who benefit from public disengagement. Real, direct personal candor closes the conversation: Thompson thanks Nate specifically for pushing her to answer questions she might not otherwise choose to address, framing the show's own real interview style as a genuine value beyond simple information exchange. She closes by directly naming her own real podcast, These Times, co-hosted with Tom McTague."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tom McTague (real, direct co-host of Thompson's own podcast, These Times, not built, given the brief namecheck nature of the citation)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_153_LukeKemp a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Luke Kemp: \"Existential Risks: The Biggest Threats to Life as We Know It\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with Luke Kemp, opening with Kemp's own real, pointed observation: no real US presidential election has ever meaningfully turned on a candidate's nuclear-weapons policy, despite a single individual, the president, holding sole real, direct authority to launch a strike — arguing this represents a genuine, structural mismatch between democratic accountability and the actual scale of catastrophic risk any single leader controls."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-11-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-12-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.LukeKemp ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/153-luke-kemp> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_194_LukeKemp ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Kemp directly distinguishes existential risk from merely catastrophic risk: the former specifically threatens humanity's long-term potential itself, not just large-scale death or suffering, arguing this real, precise distinction matters for how resources and attention should actually be allocated across different threats. He's real and direct that nuclear risk assessment has genuinely improved in some respects since the Cold War's peak, while simultaneously arguing structural safeguards have eroded in others, making net risk trajectory genuinely difficult to characterize as simply better or worse. Closes on a real, direct, live moment of forward planning rather than a simple sign-off: asked what he'd want to explore in a future conversation, Kemp names societal collapse directly, previewing his own real, forthcoming book Goliath's Curse: The History and Future of Societal Collapse — with Nate's own real, immediate, on-air proposal to record that follow-up conversation timed to the book's actual release, a real, direct, traceable origin point for what became his later TGS-194 appearance, already built in this graph."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is largely encyclopedic/institutional (nuclear command authority, existential risk taxonomy) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_154_JaneMuncke a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jane Muncke: \"Shutting Off The Plastic Tap: A Global Treaty To Regulate Petrochemical Pollution?\""@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, live special episode, recorded late at night after Nate received real, direct text messages from Muncke — an independent scientist embedded in the UN's global plastics treaty negotiations (the fifth Intergovernmental Negotiating Committee session, Busan, South Korea) — describing her real frustration as the talks adjourned without a finalized legal text, missing the original end-of-2024 target set when the UN Environment Assembly first mandated the process in March 2022. Muncke traces a real, significant shift in the negotiations' own framing over two and a half years: from an initial public narrative centered on visible plastic litter (turtles, beached whales) toward a far broader, scientifically-grounded case built substantially by independent scientists and civil society — plastic and its associated chemicals now documented in human blood and brain tissue, linked to real declines in sperm count and testosterone. The High Ambition Coalition's real core demand — reducing primary plastic polymer production at the source, not just managing waste after the fact — is presented as the negotiations' real central fault line, opposed by a 'like-minded group' of oil-producing and plastic-manufacturing states with real, direct economic interests in continued production growth."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-12-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-12-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JaneMuncke ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/154-jane-muncke> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JaSSZ7-tF60> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/TGS154JaneMunckeTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct linguistic echo of this graph's own core vocabulary surfaces unprompted: asking whether humanity's preference for short-term plastic convenience mirrors its relationship to alcohol, Nate frames the question as 'this human penchant for short-term convenience, comfort, despite the externalities' — using the term directly, independently confirming the real, direct relationship between energy blindness and economic externalities MJSullivan separately identified the same day. Muncke's own real, direct closing metaphor treats plastic dependence explicitly as a societal addiction — 'you can only do something about it when you become aware that you are addicted,' with Nate adding, in his own words, that awareness alone is only the first step; real behavior change requires consequences, support, and intervention, tying the whole discussion back to the show's own recurring dopamine-metabolism framing. Muncke is careful and direct about the real limits of her own role — 'my role in all of this is not to negotiate a legal text, but to provide independent scientific knowledge' — while still offering a real, considered account of what a genuinely successful treaty outcome would require: moving upstream from waste management and recycling toward reducing primary plastic polymer production itself. The episode closes on real, grounded hope rather than resolution: Muncke credits Thomas Homer-Dixon's 'commanding hope' framework directly, and offers her own real, personal observation that the sheer number of capable people she's met across years of INC meetings — 'little regenerative forces in themselves' — is itself a source of genuine encouragement, whatever the treaty's own eventual outcome."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Shanna Swan and Sian Sutherland, both real, directly cited TGS guests (confirmed via bare YouTube links in this episode's own show notes, no thegreatsimplification.com URL given), neither yet built; Martin Scheringer's real TGS episode (#37) is directly re-confirmed here, already a known deferred citation elsewhere in this graph. Thomas Homer-Dixon (commanding hope) is directly cited but not modeled as a Persona or Work."@en ;
    .


