tgs:Interview.TGS_158_JohnVervaeke a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "John Vervaeke: \"The Meaning Crisis: Wisdom, Purpose, and the Search for Coherence\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first-ever conversation with cognitive scientist and philosopher John Vervaeke (University of Toronto), despite years of prior familiarity with his work. Vervaeke opens by directly complicating the premise of 'the meaning crisis' as a single, simple phenomenon: real survey data shows wildly different results depending on how the question is asked (a 2019 UK survey finding a large majority reporting meaningless lives, versus academic research using validated meaning-in-life criteria finding much more positive results) — the real disagreement between these measurements, not either number alone, is itself the starting evidence for his and co-author Christopher Mastropietro's real 'symptomology of the meaning crisis' framework. Traces the crisis to a real, specific historical account: human connectedness and self-correction ('wisdom') as biologically non-optional needs, historically met through the ecologies of practice religious institutions once carried, now destabilized by secularization without adequate replacement — producing both unhealthy responses (spiritual bypassing, 'conspirituality') and healthier ones (the mindfulness revolution, a real revival of stoicism). A real, direct parallel to Nate's own biography surfaces mid-conversation: Nate's own 'Reality 101' course at the University of Minnesota included a real 'metacognition' module (cognitive biases, status, dopamine, supernormal stimuli) that was, every single year for nine years, the course's most popular unit — an independent real-world confirmation of Vervaeke's own central thesis, arrived at from a completely different direction. Closes on Vervaeke's own real, unhedged magic-wand answer: get people genuinely engaged with philosophy as a lived practice rather than an academic subject, citing Pierre Hadot, Marcus Aurelius, Shankara, and Nagarjuna as real, concrete evidence that this kind of engagement isn't 'pie in the sky.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2024-11-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-01-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JohnVervaeke ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/158-john-vervaeke> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AvgLmTkCwc4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/TGS158_JohnVervaeke.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Metacrisis ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.ZombiesInWesternCulture ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Vervaeke's own real, personal origin story anchors the whole conversation in lived rather than purely academic terms: leaving a fundamentalist Christian upbringing he describes directly as traumatizing, then a genuine, extended search — Tai Chi Chuan, Vipassana meditation, a return to Socratic and Neoplatonic philosophy — before noticing his own students consistently 'leaning forward' and 'coming on fire' whenever these threads intersected in his lectures, which he identifies as the real moment he realized this wasn't just his own idiosyncratic preoccupation. The real conceptual core — his and Mastropietro's 'symptomology' framework — treats the meaning crisis not as one thing but a real family of divergent responses (some healthy, some not) to a shared underlying loss: religion's real historical function of embedding people in traditions, communities, and trustworthy guides for cultivating wisdom, a function secularization removed without fully replacing. A real, extended historical digression on the Axial Age (following the Bronze Age collapse) is offered as Vervaeke's own preferred model for what's needed now — not a political or economic fix, but a comparable civilizational-scale transformation in how people relate to transcendence and virtue. The conversation's most personal moment comes in its final minutes: asked what he cares about most, Vervaeke draws directly on theologian Paul Tillich's individuation/participation framework, naming his children and romantic partner in the first register and the meaning crisis itself in the second — a real, structured answer rather than an offhand one."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, notable discrepancy between this episode's own show notes and its own transcript, flagged honestly rather than silently resolved: the show notes state '89% of 16-29 year olds in the UK say their life has no meaning,' but Vervaeke's own spoken words on the recording say '85 percent' of '19 to 39 year olds' from a 2019 UK survey — different percentages and different age brackets from the same underlying source, not reconciled in this pass. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Christopher Mastropietro (Vervaeke's own real co-author, cited throughout), Gregg Henriques (real, directly cited cognitive scientist), and Peter Turchin (real, directly cited historian/'immiseration' researcher), all now built (2026-09-02's broader citation network but not yet built here); Hartmut Rosa ('dynamic stabilization'), Erich Fromm, Scott Barry Kaufman (already a real, indirectly-known name via Concept.LightTriad's own origin), and Jordan Hall — all real, directly cited, none built in this pass."@en ;
    .


